Z||000000||danny boy||06-02-2001||01:06 AM|||| I did not want any part of the thread that I started this one because of. And I am going to be very vague-deal with it.<P><BR> My younger brother has paranoid schitzophrenia. No one knows exactly why, but complications at birth, severe mental duress from age six till his mind cramped and yes heavy usage of sorts are all believed to have been contributing factors.<BR>  I'm not saying whats good or bad,Its not my choice to make for anyone but me. All I know is my brother who was destined for a career in baseball suddenly ended up talking to walls and chairs and stuff. When the problem first arose , it was bad. Real bad.  <BR>He and his little *******  buddies who I had a few words with, then let a few friends scare straight(its amazing what some people will do if you tell them they get to keep a backpack full) were doing things I couldn't believe. They were breaking and entering, stealing from family and friends, and other stuff I don't want to get into. <P>When my brother was admitted to the hospital he was flying. He managed to rip a window out of his room with his bare hands,drop 3 stories onto a concrete sidewalk, get up and run away. Three days later he showed up at my moms house in his underwear and shoes, and didn't know he was even in a hospital.<BR>I don't think that was just the disease.<P> He is much better now, He has been coming out of his bedroom and doing things now. He still won't leave the house unless he double checks that all the doors are locked and he takes his CD collection with him.Even then, he won't stay gone for more than an hour or so.<BR>Theres nothing to blame and no reason to. This is the way it is and nothing is going to change that. All I know is this: I know what it is like to see your brother go from a very promising athelete to a mental case. <BR> Words cannot express how great the pain is. So to you, I can relate, but I'm sure you know that talking makes it easier, but most of the details and stories can never be told, especially here.<BR> BTW, my brother will be 21 in September.<P> Now, on to my second thing.  <P> I can't really say for sure, but I started when i was in 9th grade. By 11th grade I had almost no math skills whatsoever. Haven't done anything since I my 18th Bday. Some people it's good to, some its not. Four people I know are everyday, but still maintain a 4.0 at WVU. doesn't(hasn't) effected their mentality at all(yet).<BR>On the other hand, I have lost many of my friends because the stuff has become the only thing in their lives. Sad.<BR> So my point is ,It may be a contributing factor in whats wrong with this world, it may not. I will always think it is safer than alcohol, the lesser of two evils. Nothing like that is safe and caffeene has to be the worst of all. I personally would rather go without a cigarette than without a pepsi. Scary.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||06-02-2001||11:13 AM||||Its such a tragic thing - its almost like someone travels back in time, and something changes and when they come back their brother, who was a bright kid and had a promising future, is now severely retarded or mentally incapacitiated - like it happend overnight - or like when an older person has a severe stroke and cant function at all.<P>  the person you knew just a short time ago is gone, and there is a shadow of him still there.<P>   Ill tell you one thing, if your doctor gave your kid some perscription, and the next week or month the kid became like this, there would be a major lawsuit and millions of dollars would change hands. For people who are promoting illegal substance usage to say its not responsible for what happens to all these kids, inspite of the numbers and statistics...<P>   Well I guess, how can you expect anyone that is using the stuff to have a clear and objective opinion of what it does.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JSocha||06-02-2001||11:29 AM||||I stay away from my bro.  He not only scares me (I know its not his fault as there was nothing he may have been able to do about it) but he scares my wife and my three step daughters when he isn't taking his meds and/or he goes for the illegal stuff.  <P>It scared my oldest step daughter straight when she talked to him and he told her that he didn't start hearing voices until a few months after he started smoking p*t.  Could have been laced and he may not have known it.  He was smoking more of that then he was drinking.<P>Our family, not to sound mean and/or cruel, wish he would pass away in his sleep peacefully so he would be out of his misery with this disease.  Because when he gets bad, he gets really, really bad and ends up in the psychiatric ward where he it takes he months to recover.  <P>And of course, he is all alone in life at almost 50 yo as he is unable to find someone that understands this disease or has the disease themselves to share his life with.  Parents are also getting tired of going over to his place to get the c*rn tape out of his VCR that gets stuck in there.<P>I had an uncle who died in an instutition from the disease who did not take drugs or alcohol a day in his life.  He had the disease at a very early age.  From what my parents found out through family when my mom started checking into the disease, his started when he was about 5 yo and by 10yo he couldn't function anymore and was inside the looney bin permantly because noone knew how to handle or cope with the disease.<P>BTW, correction on the other thread:  Not "Van Gogh imagery" but "Picasso imagery" is what he sees.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
